#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.21.1,<1",
#     "packaging>=23",
# ]
# ///
"""
capabilities — the capability manager.

This tool is designed to be invoked by agents. Run `capabilities help` to load
the full usage contract on demand.

The manager is the engine of the capability system: it installs capability
scripts into the machine registry, snapshots their self-descriptions, gates
them through project/global policy, composes the session context injection, and verifies the
whole arrangement. It is the SOLE WRITER of the shared surfaces it manages —
the machine registry (~/.capabilities/<name>/), the project/global policy
collection, the project capabilities/.gitignore, the global skill
(~/.capabilities/.manager/skill/, symlinked into each host), and the
host hook wiring. Capability scripts never write any of these.

ENVIRONMENT
  CAPABILITIES_HOME    machine registry root        (default ~/.capabilities)
  CAPABILITIES_SOURCE  catalogue base URL           (default the public repo, raw)
  CAPABILITIES_BIN     PATH dir for symlinks        (default every directory already
                                                     holding the manager's own link,
                                                     on PATH or ~/.local/bin or ~/bin;
                                                     else ~/.local/bin or ~/bin,
                                                     whichever exists and is on PATH)
  CAPABILITIES_CONTEXT_PROMPT
                       optional project .env(.local) or process-env override
                       for the generated agent instruction paragraph.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ENVELOPE
                       the project envelope location, supplied by the project's
                       context owner in advance of the lookup below.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ENVELOPE_ROOT
                       the project root the value above answers for. A capability
                       sets both when it hands its resolved answer down to the
                       processes it spawns, so a descendant standing in another
                       project passes that handoff over and resolves its own.
                       Alone it carries no answer; an envelope supplied without
                       it is honored wherever it is inherited.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID
                       the project's id, its one binding: set in the process
                       environment with the root below (by a deployment, or
                       handed down beside the envelope by a process that
                       resolved it), else in the project root's .env.local,
                       else its .env. The environment wins over the files.
                       Without the root below it binds no folder.
  CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID_ROOT
                       the project root that id answers for. The project root
                       is the nearest folder, walking up from
                       CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR (else the working directory), that
                       this names beside the id, or whose .env.local or .env
                       sets the id, or that still carries an older binding
                       (capabilities/project.json or settings.json, a legacy
                       .capabilities/, .contextkit/config.toml); with none on
                       the way, the folder this names; else the nearest folder
                       holding .env, .env.local or .git, bound to no id.
  CAPABILITIES_READ_ONLY
                       `1` or `true` (any case) puts the process and everything
                       it starts under the read-only switch: every capability
                       reads but changes nothing (write verbs, project-record
                       writes and service activation exit 4), and this manager
                       refuses agent convert, agent set, allow, connections
                       import, disable, enable, inherit, init, node add, node
                       remove, node seen, node set, place (but its report),
                       quarantine, relabel, set, store set, store unset and
                       unplace.
                       doctor reports it. Unset, `0` or `false` leaves it off.
  AGENTKIT_DB_URL, AGENTKIT_DB_HOST, AGENTKIT_DB_PORT, AGENTKIT_DB_NAME,
  AGENTKIT_DB_USER, AGENTKIT_DB_PASSWORD, AGENTKIT_DB_SCHEMA,
  AGENTKIT_DB_SSLMODE, AGENTKIT_DB_SSLROOTCERT, AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_HOST,
  AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_PORT
                       the environment level of the database cascade, below a
                       project's .env.local/.env and above the machine's store
                       setting (see STORE VERBS).

PROJECT PATH VERB
  path --json [--identity]
        Resolve the current project root and capability envelope once, returning
        project_root, project_envelope, and provider. Plain projects use
        <root>/<body root>/capabilities, the body root their agent's row
        names (see THE PROJECT ENVELOPE). ContextKit-bound projects must
        return a usable absolute in-project path; a broken binding is an
        error, never a fallback.
        body_root and layers name the agent's body: each of context, assets,
        routines and capabilities as the absolute path of its folder, except
        a layer the agent keeps in its store, decided as `build` decides it,
        which is {where: "store", read, folder}: read the command that lists
        it (context list, assets list, routine list --store), folder where its
        import and export default to. While the store does not answer, each
        layer is its folder.
        --identity adds project_identity: the project id
        (CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID) and its state - handed (the process
        environment's, for this root), declared (the project root's
        .env.local, else .env, holds it) or undeclared (no id) - with bound (a
        .contextkit/config.toml is present) and contextkit_id (the id where
        bound, else null), the two fields capabilities stamped with an earlier
        contract read. Where the env files declare no id and the envelope's
        project.json still holds one (schema v1), or else
        .contextkit/config.toml holds one under [identity], that id is moved
        into .env.local once and leaves project.json (not under the read-only
        switch) and answered.

THE PROJECT ENVELOPE
  Every project file the system owns — identity, the gate, the state guard,
  and each capability's identifiers, references, connections, settings and
  state — sits in one envelope; records are kept in files, except the
  connections, grants, identifiers and policy of a project in store mode,
  which its store holds (see `connections import`), and the references
  a project imported into its store (see `references`). In a project
  bound by `.contextkit/config.toml` its location is read from that binding:
  `[body] root`, the folder holding the agent's body, and `[sources]
  capabilities` inside it (`.capabilities` names the legacy project-root
  envelope). Without that binding the envelope is
  <root>/<body_root>/capabilities, the body root the agent's row names
  (capabilities_agents.body_root), and <root>/capabilities where it names
  none, the project has no id or no store is configured for it. The row is
  read when the manager's envelope record holds no standing answer for the
  root; while the store does not answer, the last answer recorded for the
  root and its id stands whatever its age, else an existing
  <root>/capabilities folder is the envelope, else the call exits 5
  store_unreachable. A binding that names an absolute, hidden or
  out-of-project path is an error (body_path_invalid); it never silently
  selects another tree. `path --json` reports the body root and each layer.

MACHINE VERBS
  install <name> [--from <path|url> | --source ID] [--yes] [--allow]
        Fetch the capability into the registry, symlink its executable onto
        PATH, snapshot `stub` + `manifest --json` (keyed by payload hash),
        scaffold credentials per the declared scope, surface declared
        post-install steps (--yes runs them), migrate its tables (see
        update), regenerate the skill.
        A capability new to this machine arrives quarantined (see allow);
        --allow installs it allowed. A core capability (see list) new to this
        machine and installed from the official catalogue - no --from, no
        --source - arrives allowed and enabled at global scope instead: as the
        store's global policy row (every agent, every node) where a database
        resolves, unless the store already holds a global row for it, which is
        kept, or the process runs under CAPABILITIES_READ_ONLY, which opens no
        gate. Install writes no policy file anywhere: where no database
        resolves or answers, nothing is written and the enable is reported
        pending, for `capabilities enable <name> --global` on the master. JSON
        then carries global_policy {enabled, gate} naming the store's table,
        or {enabled, pending, reason, hint}. Reinstalling or updating an
        installed capability keeps its machine state and writes no policy;
        uninstall drops the machine state.
        --from accepts: a script file, a bundle directory holding <name> or
        bin/<name>, a repo directory holding capabilities/<name>/bin/<name>, or
        a URL. Source directories, and script paths inside source directories,
        install the complete bundle, excluding bytecode/cache files. Default
        catalogue sources install from the public repo bundle when available.
        --source installs from one explicitly named registered source and records
        its repository/ref/commit provenance. An unqualified name remains an
        explicit request for the built-in official catalogue; custom sources
        never shadow it.
  uninstall <name> [--purge-credentials]
        Remove the PATH symlink and the registry entry; regenerate the skill.
        Credentials are kept and reported; --purge-credentials deletes the
        user-scope credential file after showing it.
  update [<name>]
        Re-fetch one capability (or all) from its recorded source; refresh
        snapshots; regenerate the skill.
        Install, update and the release that reconciles this machine bring
        capabilities-contract in each capability's environment to the newest
        release its declared range admits (uv sync --script), so a release of
        the shared code reaches an installed capability without an edit to
        it; where uv cannot, a warning says so and the capability runs on the
        library it has.
        Install, update and the release that reconciles this machine run
        `<name> migrate` once the payload is in place, when its manifest
        declares tables in the machine's store, and report it as `migrate`
        {ok, applied, version} or {ok: false, exit, error}. A refusal - no
        store configured, store unreachable or busy, the capability
        quarantined - leaves the payload installed; its first call migrates.
  update --check [<name>] [--json]
        Report, for every installed capability or the one named, whether its
        recorded source carries a different payload. Writes no registry entry,
        snapshot or skill; the only thing it may bring forward is a registered
        source's cache, the tree `search` reads. Payloads are compared by their
        catalogue hash - the generated catalogue's payload_sha256, recomputed for
        the installed copy - and versions are the newest heading of each change
        log, null where none can be read. JSON {checked: [{name, source,
        installed {catalog_sha256, version, source_commit?},
        available {catalog_sha256, version} | null, update_available,
        error? {code, message, hint?}}], update_available}; the top-level
        update_available is true when any row's is. A source that cannot be
        reached or read leaves that row's available null and update_available
        false and names it in error; the other rows are still answered. Exit 0
        when every row was answered, behind or current alike; 5 when any row
        carries an error; 3 when the named capability is not installed. Output
        is always JSON, so --json changes nothing.
  self-update [--from <checkout>]
        Refresh the manager itself and re-point its PATH symlink (install.sh is
        the first-install bootstrap). By default fetch the canonical source;
        --from installs the manager and release assets from a local checkout.
        The incoming release manifest owns the asset list and hashes; the whole
        release is verified and staged before one atomic symlink switch. No-op
        when already current. The manager is not a capability, so it has no
        registry entry or snapshots.
  self-update --check [--from <checkout>] [--json]
        Report whether the canonical source (or --from) carries a manager
        release other than the one the PATH symlink selects. Reads only the
        incoming release manifest; stages, writes and switches nothing. A
        release is identified by its release id, the sha256 of its release
        manifest, which is the name self-update gives its release directory;
        the version is the newest heading of that release's manager change log,
        null where none can be read. JSON {installed {release, manager_sha256,
        version} | null, available {release, manager_sha256, version, source},
        update_available}; installed is null when the PATH symlink selects no
        versioned release, which self-update replaces. Exit 0 when the check
        answered, behind or current alike; 5 when the source cannot be reached
        or carries no valid release manifest. Output is always JSON, so --json
        changes nothing.
  allow <name> / quarantine <name>
        Set this machine's ceiling for an installed capability, kept in
        $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/machine.json, which only the manager
        writes. Quarantined is effective-disabled in every project and outside
        any, whatever project or global policy says; allowed leaves the two
        scopes deciding. A capability with no entry is allowed. JSON
        {capability, machine: "allowed"|"quarantined", changed}. Exit 3 when
        not installed. Lifting a quarantine is the user's decision: an agent
        asks and never runs `allow` on its own initiative.
  skill
        Place the carried global skill and relink every agent host present on
        this machine. The bootstrap and every install/uninstall/update already
        do this; run it after installing a new agent host, or to repair the
        drift `doctor` reports.
  list
        Installed capabilities with summaries, tier, and explicit project/global
        gate, effective state, the scope that supplied it, and — in a project —
        the resolved envelope path. `tier` is on every row, "core" or
        "standard": the manager declares the catalogue's core set once, as
        CORE_CAPABILITIES in its own executable, and every other capability is
        standard. The tier is a mark to group by, and it acts only at first
        arrival (see install): there a core capability arrives allowed and
        enabled at global scope. After that it is gated, enabled and granted
        exactly as a standard one; a bundled service still needs an explicit
        project enable, and a connection still needs its grant. Source
        verification refuses a core name the catalogue does not carry. Every
        row carries `machine` ("allowed" or
        "quarantined") and `machine_connections`, the connections this
        machine's global scope declares for it less those whose grant there
        is disabled - once the store's global rows exist, the shared
        connections the store holds for it, with a config-home file counted
        only for one the store holds no row of; a quarantined row is
        effective "disabled" with source "machine".
  search [QUERY] [--source ID]
        Search cached catalogues from the official and registered sources.
        Listing never executes catalogue capability code. JSON {matches [{name,
        source, summary, installed}], unavailable_sources [{source, hint}]}.
        Every row carries installed: true when a capability of that name is in
        the machine registry, false when it is a catalogue capability not yet
        installed here.
  changelog (<name> | --manager | --contract) [--all]
            [--from <path> | --source ID]
        Print the change log of a capability, of the manager, or of the
        contract: what changed for someone who already has it. By default only
        the entries newer than the installed version; --all prints the whole
        log. The installed version is the newest entry in the installed copy's
        own log - the registry copy for a capability, the manager release on
        PATH for the manager and the contract. An installed copy without a log
        predates change logs, so every entry is newer than it; a capability not
        installed here prints its whole log. The log is read from where the
        update would come from: a capability's recorded source, else the
        official catalogue, and the manager's own source for the manager and
        the contract. --source reads one registered source's local tree, as
        `search` does (`source refresh` brings it forward); --from reads a
        local checkout or bundle. Read-only: it installs and writes nothing.
        JSON {target, kind, log, installed {version, log,
        registry|release, source_commit?} | null, view, entries [{heading,
        body}], note?}. Exit 3 when the source carries no log, 5 when it
        cannot be reached.
  source init ID [--remote GIT_URL]
        Create the canonical editable source workspace at
        ~/.capabilities/sources/ID, initialize git, copy the authoring law,
        register it, and emit the exact authoring/publishing path.
  source add ID GIT_URL [--ref REF]
        Register and fetch a remote capability source into the manager-owned
        read-only cache. Existing git/SSH credentials are used; no token is stored.
  source clone ID GIT_URL [--ref REF]
        Clone an existing source into the canonical editable workspace and
        register it for authoring on this machine.
  source register-checkout official
        Register this exact primary official checkout for consumer-originated
        development. Must be invoked as ./bin/capabilities from that checkout.
  source list | path ID | refresh [ID] | sync ID | check ID | remove ID
        Inspect sources, resolve the one editable path, refresh remote caches,
        compare declarations, payload hashes and the generated catalogue, or
        unregister a source. Authoring audit stays explicit.
        `check` writes nothing, so it reads any registered source — an editable
        workspace or a fetched cache — from any directory. Exit 0 clean, 7 drift.
        Removing never deletes an editable workspace.
  source index ID [--staged]
        Deterministically rebuild the generated catalogue. --staged derives it
        from the Git index and stages the result, so ignored, unstaged, and
        temporary files cannot influence the candidate commit. It never audits.
  source verify ID --ref REF [--base REF]
        Verify one immutable Git tree. With --base, derive the direct manager and
        capability checks from the diff; without it, use the full safe fallback.
        Each selected capability is audited once. This is the publication/CI invariant.
  source release ID --ref REF
        Publish one already-committed, verified descendant of the clean source
        checkout's tracked branch; fast-forward the checkout, reconcile locally
        installed changed payloads, and update the manager when it changed.

STORE VERBS
  Which PostgreSQL a project's capabilities keep their tables in is resolved
  per project through one cascade, by the shared database library
  capabilities-contract (capabilities_contract.db) and nowhere else; the
  first level that answers is used whole:
    project      the AGENTKIT_DB_* keys in the project's .env.local and .env,
                 .env.local winning key by key;
    environment  the same keys in the process environment;
    machine      this machine's store setting file,
                 $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentkit/store.json
                 (~/.config/agentkit/store.json when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset),
                 an agentkit.store.v1 document holding host, port, database,
                 user, password, sslmode, an optional sslrootcert, an
                 optional db_schema (default agentkit) and an optional
                 transaction_host and transaction_port, at mode 0600. Its
                 format is SHEBANG.md's "The store setting".
  Within the project or environment level AGENTKIT_DB_URL, a postgresql://
  URL, wins over that level's separate keys; AGENTKIT_DB_SCHEMA and the
  transaction address apply beside it.
  A store has one address unless the level in force also names a transaction
  address (AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_HOST and AGENTKIT_DB_TRANSACTION_PORT, or
  transaction_host and transaction_port in the file): the same store at
  another endpoint, typically a transaction-mode pooler, with the level's own
  credentials, database and schema; one naming only a port keeps the host.
  Then only the processes that hold LISTEN - a service's listener, a watch -
  connect to the address; every other connection - each command, the
  records reader, a lease renewal, a service's questions - goes to the
  transaction address and carries no session state. A hosted store with a
  session-mode and a transaction-mode pooler on one host takes the session
  pooler as its address and the transaction pooler's port as
  --transaction-port; a known transaction pooler as the address itself is
  refused (exit 6 transaction_pooler). An agent's records - its gate,
  connections, grants, identifiers and settings - are files only until it
  has moved into the store (see migrate); from then they are rows of the
  contract's tables in the store, beside the tables each capability migrates
  for itself. These verbs never create a table.
  store set --host H --database D --user U [--port P]
            [--sslmode require|verify-ca|verify-full|disable]
            [--sslrootcert PATH|system] [--schema NAME]
            [--transaction-host H] [--transaction-port P]
            (--password-stdin | --password-file PATH | --password-env KEY)
        Write the machine level: the setting file whole (a temporary file
        renamed over it, mode 0600) under the manager's lock, in the format
        the library reads, which is proven before the file is replaced. A
        file declaring a format version this manager does not know is left
        as it is (exit 6 store_setting_too_new). The password comes from
        stdin, a file or the named environment variable, never from argv; a
        --password flag is refused. --port defaults to 5432; --sslmode
        defaults to verify-full with --sslrootcert and to require without it;
        disable is admitted only for a local host (a Unix socket directory,
        localhost or a loopback address), and disable elsewhere, allow and
        prefer are refused (exit 6 sslmode_too_weak). --schema names the
        schema the setting binds: a lowercase identifier, never public,
        information_schema or pg_* (exit 6 bad_schema_name); without it the
        file names none and binds agentkit. --transaction-host and
        --transaction-port name the store's transaction address (either one
        alone keeps the other from the address); without them the store has
        one address. Exit 4 under the read-only
        switch. Answers as `store show` does for the current project, with
        changed.
  store show [--project PATH] [--json]
        Every level of the cascade for the current project, or for the
        project at PATH: what each level holds, its secrets redacted (a
        password as ***, a URL as the parameters libpq reads from it, never
        its text), the files or variables its values came from, and which
        level is in force. Reads only. JSON {configured, project, in_force,
        setting, error, levels {project {asked, answers, setting, error},
        environment {answers, setting, error}, machine {path, exists,
        answers, setting, error}}}: setting is the library's report of the
        level in force {level, sources, schema, transaction {host, port}?,
        url{...} | host, port, database, user, sslmode, sslrootcert,
        password}; error {code,
        message, hint} names a level the library refuses. Output is always
        JSON, so --json changes nothing.
  store doctor [--project PATH] [--json]
        Prove the database in force for the current project, or for the
        project at PATH, whichever level answered: a TLS connection works,
        reporting the server version and the negotiated TLS; a plain-text
        connection to the same server is refused, so a pg_hba.conf that lets
        plain text back in is noticed; and the role may use the setting's
        schema - USAGE and CREATE on it when it exists, CREATE on the
        database when it does not yet, since the first migration creates it.
        A database on a local host is not reached across a network: its
        plain-text check is skipped (skipped true), and with sslmode disable
        a working connection without TLS passes the tls check. A store
        that names a transaction address is also proven there: a transaction
        connection opened at it, over TLS unless sslmode is disable, runs a
        transaction bound to the setting's schema; a store with one address
        skips that check. Never prints a secret. JSON {ok, project,
        in_force, setting, levels, sslmode, db_schema, checks {tls {ok,
        server_version?, tls {version, cipher}?, reason?, error?},
        plain_text_refused {ok, reason, skipped?}, schema {ok, name, exists,
        reason}, transaction {ok, skipped?, host?, port?, tls?, reason?,
        error?}}}, setting and levels as `store show`.
        Exit 0 when all four hold, 7 when any fails, 3 no_store_setting when
        no level answers, 6 with the library's code when the level in force
        is refused.
  store unset
        Remove the machine level's setting file under the manager's lock. A
        file of a format version this manager does not know is left as it is
        (exit 6 store_setting_too_new). Exit 4 under the read-only switch.
        Answers as `store show` does, with changed; removing no setting is
        not an error.

DEVELOPMENT VERBS
  dev start <name> [--project PATH | --no-project] [--source ID-OR-PATH]
            [--session ID] [--allow-parallel]
        Create one isolated source worktree and attach the existing consumer
        checkout when run from it. The project is recorded but never owned,
        branched, cleaned or removed by the manager.
  dev install <session> <name> [<name> ...]
        Structurally validate and install capabilities from the session source
        worktree into its isolated registry. Re-run after source edits to refresh payloads.
  dev exec <session> [--cwd source] [--inherit-env KEY ...] -- <command>
        Execute one foreground command inside the session environment. Output
        and exit status are passed through. Parent env is denied by default;
        named keys may be inherited without putting values on argv. Where this
        machine resolves a database outside any project (its environment, then
        its store setting file), the session reaches that database bound to a
        schema of its own, dev_<session>, through the AGENTKIT_DB_* keys with
        AGENTKIT_DB_SCHEMA (which then cannot be inherited), and this
        machine's node id goes with it as AGENT_NODE_ID (with AGENT_NODE_NAME
        where set); without one the lane has no database, as before. The
        session creates that schema, marked as its own, before anything in
        the lane reaches it, and drops it when it ends - by finish, stop or
        gc - unless the schema was already there or no longer carries its
        mark; `database_schema` in the answer says which.
  dev run <session> <capability> [--project-only] -- [<arg> ...]
        Run exactly one session-installed capability against the attached live
        project through a mode-0700, per-invocation selected-input projection.
        For a project that reads its records from the store, where a database
        resolves, the projection is that capability's rows - the agent's
        registry row and nodes, its own and the global policy, connections,
        grants, identifiers and settings - copied into the session's schema,
        which the payload reads instead of the store's; otherwise a temporary
        config home with the selected gate and connection files.
  dev check <session>
        Derive validation scope from the recorded base across committed,
        staged, unstaged and untracked changes, then run each selected direct
        validator once without editing source or index.
  dev live start|status|stop <session> telegram
  dev live recover <session> telegram --restore-canonical
        Borrow one account's real Telegram state under a durable exclusive
        lease; recovery is explicit and always targets the recorded canonical state.
        Where the project names no database of its own, so the payload keeps
        its records in the session's schema, the service's register in the
        store (telegram_channels) is borrowed too: the canonical rows of the
        project's connection are copied into the session schema before the
        payload starts, and back before the canonical service restarts.
  dev doctor <session> | dev list
        Inspect provenance, worktrees, isolation paths, installed payloads, and
        cleanup readiness. Source dirtiness is visible development state, not
        by itself a failure.
  dev stop <session> [--discard-work]
        Remove only a clean session whose commits are absent, merged, or
        patch-equivalent to commits on its recorded base ref. Dirty or
        unpublished worktrees are preserved and refused. --discard-work
        removes an abandoned session by destroying exactly that work, its
        uncommitted changes and its unpublished commits, and names what it
        destroyed in the result. It never reaches a live takeover lease, a
        worktree git cannot report on, or an unmanaged path.
  dev finish <session>
        Require a clean prepared commit, release it through the existing
        source transaction, then remove only manager-owned session paths. The
        attached project is never cleaned, committed, published or removed.
  dev gc [--older-than 7d]
        Apply the same safe cleanup rule to old sessions; unsafe sessions are
        reported and retained.

POLICY VERBS
  enable <name> --project|--global [--node <node>]
  disable <name> --project|--global [--node <node>] [--deny]
        Write an explicit policy record at project or global scope through the
        active records backend. Files mode maps those scopes to
        capabilities/settings.json and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/settings.json;
        store mode to this project's row, stamped with its verified id, and the
        global row of capabilities_policy, which applies to every project
        reading that store, each for every node, or with --node for that one
        node (its id, as `node id` answers it on that machine). Outside any
        project --global writes that global row, not the
        config home's file, once the store this machine reads holds the global
        rows (global_migrated_at), and a store that does not answer then
        refuses it (exit 5). A row naming this node is read before the row for every node,
        at either scope. A row says two things apart: whether the CLI is usable
        there (enabled) and, for the project's row naming a node only, whether
        the capability's bundled service is placed on that node (service).
        enable --node writes both true, so it places the service there, and
        disable --node both false, so it also takes the service off that node
        and says so (unplaced, warning); `place` and `unplace` below change the
        service alone. A row for every node, or a global row, makes the CLI
        usable without placing the service (`deployment guide profiles`).
        Files carry no node, so --node in files mode is refused with
        node_requires_store; `capabilities connections import` moves the
        project's records into its store first. --deny takes no --node.
        Scope is mandatory: ask whether the decision is for this project or
        every project before invoking the manager. Project enable removes any
        harness deny rule; project disable --deny additionally writes
        permissions.deny Bash(<name>:*) into .claude/settings.json. Enabling a
        capability quarantined on this machine writes the entry and warns: it
        stays effective-disabled until the user allows it.
  inherit <name> --project|--global [--node <node>]
        Remove the selected scope's explicit entry. A project then inherits the
        machine default; removing the global entry restores default deny. With
        --node (store mode only) remove that scope's row for that one node;
        where that row placed the service, it stops there and the answer says
        so (unplaced, warning).
  place <name> --node <node>
  unplace <name> --node <node>
        Store mode only: put this project's bundled service of <name> on that
        node, or take it off, changing nothing about where <name> is usable.
        A service's descriptor declares how many nodes run it at once
        (service.placement): exclusive, the default, runs on one node at a
        time, so place takes it off every other node in the same write - this
        is how a service moves, its CLI left usable on the node it left;
        shared runs on every node it is placed on. Placing where the project
        has no row naming the node writes one that also enables <name> there
        (enabled_on_node), since a service runs only where its project
        enables it; placing where that row disables it is refused (exit 6
        disabled_on_node). Unplacing a node it is not on changes nothing.
        JSON {placed|unplaced, node, placement, changed, placed_on [nodes],
        unplaced_from?, enabled_on_node?, gate}. Exit 3 when <name> is not
        installed, 6 no_service when it bundles none.
  place <name> --all
        Put a shared service on every node of this project's agent (`node
        list`); an exclusive one is refused (exit 6 placement_exclusive).
        Every node is checked before anything is written: where any would
        refuse - disabled there, or a row this node may not write - nothing
        is written and the error's detail.refused names each node, code and
        message. Then it is placed node by node, each its own write; a write
        that fails part-way exits non-zero with detail {placed [nodes],
        failed}, and running the command again finishes it. Exit 6 no_nodes
        when the agent has no registered node. JSON {placed, nodes [nodes],
        placement, changed, placed_on [nodes], enabled_on_nodes?, gate}.
  place <name>
        Store mode only, and allowed under the read-only switch: where the
        service is placed beside the node running it, writing nothing. JSON
        {capability, placement, placed_on [nodes], holder, lease {node,
        acquired_at, renewed_at, expires_at, live} | null, agrees, gate}.
        holder is the node whose lease is live (deployment's runner takes it
        on the node the service is placed on), else null; agrees is whether
        holder is one of placed_on, or with nothing placed whether no node
        holds it, so false while a move is under way until the lease names
        the new node. A
        shared service takes no lease: holder and lease are null and agrees
        is null.

PROJECT VERBS (run inside a consuming project)
  init [--claude] [--codex] [--with-layers | --with-template | --id <id>]
        Wire the project: bind it to its id - CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID in the
        root's .env.local, which is the whole binding (init writes no
        capabilities/project.json; one an older init wrote is kept, without
        an id, until the project moves) - and scaffold the manager-owned
        capabilities/.gitignore, and settings.json only where an older
        project.json keeps the project in files mode. A new project - no
        older project.json, no settings file, no legacy envelope, and no id
        the store already holds an agent for - is a new agent and starts in
        the store: its row is registered migrated with this node as master,
        and init refuses it, writing nothing, where this machine resolves no
        node id (node_unidentified, bad_node_id; see `node id`), without a
        store (store_not_configured), or on a store
        whose global records have not moved (global_not_migrated). An
        existing project is initialised as before. The id is written once and
        never rewritten: an id the project already resolves (the environment,
        its env files, or a project.json v1 it is moved out of) is kept, an
        id `.contextkit/config.toml` holds under [identity] is carried over,
        otherwise one is minted offline, twelve lowercase hex characters
        (`agent rekey` moves an agent off an older form) - except where
        nothing names the id in a checkout of an existing project (a v2 project.json, or the
        envelope's .gitignore, without an id: a fresh clone): init then
        refuses (no_project_identity) and asks for --id. A registered row
        without a slug is given one: the slug an older project.json names,
        else the agent's name transliterated, else the folder's name (the
        result's agent.slug; see relabel). --id <id> binds the folder to the
        agent the store holds under that id (twelve hex characters, prj_ and
        twelve, or a UUID; exit 6 input otherwise) instead of creating one: where a
        database resolves for the folder the agent must be registered there
        (exit 6 agent_unknown) and an older project.json of a moved agent
        says "records": "store"; with no database it is bound unverified. It
        writes the id to .env.local and nothing else of the agent, refuses
        an id other than one the folder already resolves (project_id_exists),
        and answers raised {id, verified, migrated, name, store} or {id,
        verified: false, reason}. Run once in each clone of a
        repository: the id is not committed. When a database resolves for the
        project, its agent row is registered in capabilities_agents (the
        result's `agent`: registered, store_not_configured, or the store's
        error, which never fails init), and the row of this node (its id,
        `node id`) among its nodes is given the folder as its
        `folder` where it records none and no other agent's row holds that
        folder on this node; per host, wire the
        SessionStart hook and the generated context file and generate the
        skill. No flags defaults to Claude Code wiring; Codex projects must
        pass --codex. Host wiring is manager-owned: each init brings the hook
        entries and Codex config keys it owns to their current form, and
        refuses a host settings file it cannot parse rather than replace it.
        Idempotent. When `.contextkit/config.toml` is present, init keeps the
        envelope and skill setup but skips host wiring: ContextKit owns it.
        Without one, init --id wires the agent's hosts as `hooks install`
        does - the hosts its flags name, Claude when none - instead of the
        standalone wiring, and answers the result as `hooks`, a build that
        fails as hooks.build.error; plain init does the same for a host
        whose SessionStart hook already runs `capabilities session-start`
        (with no flag, for every such host), and wires the standalone
        context only on the other hosts it names.
        A legacy project `.capabilities/` envelope is preflighted and migrated
        into visible `capabilities/`; content conflicts refuse before writes.
        --with-layers first lays, in the folder init runs in, the body
        ContextKit's `init --with-layers` lays - .contextkit/config.toml,
        .contextkit/README.md, .gitignore lines, .env.local and the body
        layer folders, each written only where missing - and
        --with-template adds ContextKit's starter context files; the answer
        adds {body_root, created, changed, skipped}.
  init --template <id> [--name <name>]
        Create a new agent in an empty folder (a .git or .DS_Store aside)
        from one of ContextKit's agent templates, as `contextkit init
        --template` does: git init, the body --with-layers lays (through the
        new-agent path above, so it starts in the store and is refused
        there), the template's files, its icon (.contextkit/icon-<sha>.png)
        and name - --name, or one the template offers - description and
        onboarding in the agent row, its capabilities enabled for the
        project and confirmed through `list`, both hosts wired by `hooks
        install`, and the first build. Answers {project, created, template,
        ok, git, identity, onboarding, capabilities [{name, result,
        effective, detail}], hooks, build}; exits 1 with the agent created
        when a capability could not be enabled. Exit 6 folder_not_empty,
        unknown_template, template_invalid.
  templates [--json]
        What `contextkit templates --json` answers: {source, starter [{path,
        title, description}], agents [{id, name, description, icon,
        capabilities, routines, onboarding, agent_names, path}]}. Templates
        are read from the agenttemplates capability's installed bundle
        (~/.capabilities/agenttemplates/templates) when it is installed, and
        otherwise where ContextKit reads them: the folder
        CONTEXTKIT_TEMPLATES_DIR names, beside the `contextkit` on PATH (its
        install or checkout), then ~/.contextkit/templates; exit 6
        templates_not_found when none is there. `init --with-template` and
        `init --template` read the same folder.
  identity set [--name <n>] [--description <d>] [--icon <png>] [--project <dir>]
        In a ContextKit-bound project, write the agent's name, description or
        icon to its row as `agent set` does, checked as ContextKit checks
        them (one line; name 120, description 500 characters; a square PNG of
        64-1024 pixels, kept as .contextkit/icon-<sha>.png, other such icons
        removed).
  bootstrap [--target claude|codex]... [--yes]
        Bind the project as `contextkit bootstrap` does: a Git repository
        (where the folder holds files and none, a checkpoint commit of them
        first, which needs --yes), `init --with-layers`, `hooks install` for
        each target (both by default) and the build. Answers {project,
        targets, git, init, hooks, build}; ContextKit's doctor and audit are
        not run.
  relabel <slug>
        Give this project a new slug, keeping its id: the slug is its agent's
        row in the store (capabilities_agents.slug, unique there: exit 6
        slug_taken), registered first where it is not, and an older
        capabilities/project.json that still names one is kept saying the
        same. Without a database the slug is written to that project.json,
        and refused (exit 5 store_not_configured) where there is none. Records
        are not moved and are not re-scoped — they never referred to the
        slug. Renaming the directory is unrelated and needs no command.
        Answers {ok, project, id, slug, previous_slug, identity (where it was
        written), unchanged}. Idempotent.
  agent show [--icon-out PATH]
        This project's row in the agent registry (capabilities_agents in the
        project's store, keyed by the project id): {ok, project,
        agent_id, registered, agent, store {level, schema}}, where agent is
        null until the agent is registered, else {id, slug, name, description,
        icon_sha, icon_bytes, onboarding_status, onboarding_routine,
        body_root, collaboration_mode, migrating_since, migrated_at,
        created_at, updated_at}: body_root is the folder holding the body of
        an agent without a ContextKit binding (null: the project root),
        collaboration_mode ask or direct (null: ask). --icon-out
        writes the icon's PNG bytes to PATH (exit 6 no_icon without one).
  agent set [--name TEXT] [--description TEXT] [--icon PNG | --no-icon]
            [--onboarding-status pending|complete|none]
            [--onboarding-routine NAME] [--collaboration-mode ask|direct]
        Register the agent where it is not yet registered and write the
        fields given; an empty TEXT or NAME, `none` and --no-icon clear a
        field. --icon takes a PNG file and stores it with its SHA-256.
        --collaboration-mode is what `build` composes for an agent without
        a ContextKit binding (ask: the Collaboration Mode text).
        Answers {ok, project, agent_id, created, changed, agent, store};
        writing the values a row already holds changes nothing.
  agent list
        Every agent the store holds - the project's store inside a project,
        else the one this machine resolves - each as `agent show` answers it
        with its nodes as `node list` answers them, the folder it lives in on
        each node among them: {ok, agents [{id, slug, name, ..., nodes
        [{node, kind, ..., folder}]}], store}. Needs no project.
  agent state <id|slug>
        One read of an agent the store holds across its nodes, writing
        nothing (allowed under the read-only switch; needs no project):
        {ok, agent_id, agent, this_node, liveness {by, folder,
        live_within_seconds, reason}, nodes [{...node list's fields...,
        address, address_source (node | coolify | null), address_error?,
        seen_seconds_ago, live, gate {<capability>: {effective, source
        (project | global | machine | default), node}}, placed [capability],
        leases [{capability, acquired_at, renewed_at, expires_at}]}],
        services [{capability, placement, nodes, placed_on, holders}],
        store}. address is the node's row's, else the ip of the Coolify
        server it was created on (`coolify servers`, through its
        connection). live is deployment's answer (`deployment node list`
        in the agent's folder on this machine); where deployment is not
        installed or the agent has no folder here it is null and
        liveness.reason says why. A gate is read from the store's policy
        rows highest first - the agent's row for the node, its row for every
        node, the global rows likewise (node names the node a row was for),
        this machine's ceiling on this node alone - and is disabled by
        default; it names every capability a row names. placed is the
        services placed on the node (`place`), leases the live leases it
        holds in deployment_service_leases. services lists each capability
        with a service installed here or placed on a node: placement from its
        descriptor (null where not installed here), nodes its declared
        single | several (null until declared). Exit 6 agent_unknown for an
        agent the store does not hold.
  agent materialize <id|slug> <folder> [--claude] [--codex]
        Put an agent the store holds in a folder on this machine: register
        this machine by its node id (`node id`; exit 6 node_unidentified
        where it resolves none) among its nodes with that folder (a node new
        to the registry is labelled as this machine labels itself and is
        kind mac on a Mac, else server; the link is neither master nor
        writing), write CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID
        and CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID_ROOT into the folder's .env.local
        (creating the folder where it is missing), then bind, wire and build
        the folder as `init --id` does - Claude when no host is named. It
        refuses before writing anything an agent the store does not hold
        (agent_unknown), a folder bound to another agent by its env files,
        an older binding or the environment, or recorded as another agent's
        folder on this node (folder_bound; the store holds one agent per
        folder per node), and an agent that already lives in another folder
        here which is still bound to it (agent_elsewhere; one whose folder
        is gone or bound elsewhere is moved). Where the wiring after those
        writes is refused, the .env.local, this node's row and the folders it
        made are put back as they were. Run again it changes nothing,
        and run in a folder already bound to the agent it records the folder
        on this node's row. Answers {ok, agent_id, folder, node, changed, env
        {path, changed}, init, store}, init being what `init --id` answers.
        `capabilities node remove <node>` in the folder removes the node
        again; the folder's files stay. Exit 4 under the read-only switch.
  agent convert
        Turn a ContextKit-bound agent into one bound by its id alone, as one
        change. It first checks, writing nothing when it refuses: the id
        resolves (an [identity] id is moved into .env.local), a store
        answers and holds the agent's row (store_not_configured,
        store_unreachable, agent_unknown), the config is valid
        (contextkit_config_invalid) and sets no [sources] entry (exit 6
        setting_retired, naming it: global context lives in the store
        through `context`), and .contextkit/ holds only config.toml,
        README.md, generated/ with generated context alone, and
        icon-*.png (exit 6 contextkit_folder_not_empty, naming the rest;
        icon_ambiguous for more than one icon the row would take). It then
        carries [body] root into the row's body_root (null for .),
        [collaboration] mode into collaboration_mode where set, [onboarding]
        into the onboarding columns and the icon into the row where the row
        has none; builds the context at .capabilities-generated/context.md,
        points every wired host's link at it and brings the wired hosts'
        hooks to `capabilities session-start` as `hooks install` does;
        removes the old generated output; writes the manager's block of
        host files into the root .gitignore; in a Git repository removes
        those host files and every tracked file under .contextkit/ from the
        index, keeping the host files on disk, and stages the .gitignore,
        committing nothing; and removes .contextkit/. A refusal while it
        builds (a context file the build refuses, a file it did not
        generate at the new output) puts the binding, the host files, the
        .gitignore and the row's values back as it found them. Answers {ok, project,
        agent_id, changed, carried, removed, untracked, staged, wrote,
        hosts, build, agent}. Run where no ContextKit binding is left, it
        changes nothing and answers changed false with a message. Exit 4
        under the read-only switch.
  agent rekey [<new id>] [--dry-run]
        Move this project's agent from the id it is bound to to a new one:
        the id given (twelve lowercase hex characters, the form every new
        agent is given; exit 6 input otherwise) or one minted, which must be
        held nowhere in the store (exit 6 id_in_use). In one transaction it
        rewrites every row of the store's schema whose text column holds the
        old id whole - any table, any column; text that only contains it is
        history and stays - with the tables' triggers that would refuse the
        move held off inside that transaction, and rolls back where a row
        still holds the old id (exit 5 rekey_incomplete). It refuses,
        changing nothing, while the agent's services run (exit 6
        agent_services_running: an unexpired lease row in the store naming
        the id, or a machine service's project list on this machine keying
        it, each named with how to stop it), and an agent holding asset
        files, whose bucket objects are stored under the id (exit 6
        rekey_assets_files). Then it rebinds each node it can reach: the
        env file line of the folder it runs in (only that folder: another
        one this node's row records is named), and a server node created through a coolify connection by setting
        CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID in its resource's environment with `coolify
        env set` (it starts under the new id at its next deploy). Every
        other node is named in `unreached` with what to run there: on a Mac,
        `capabilities agent rekey <new id>` in the agent's folder, which
        finds the store already moved and rebinds that folder alone. An id
        the environment hands this folder is named, not written.
        --dry-run changes nothing and answers the same, with every row
        (each table, column, count, and the rows' keys: the primary key,
        else the first unique index), the
        triggers, the files and resources it would write, the services
        running and what would refuse. Answers {ok, project, dry_run,
        old_id, new_id, minted, store {level, schema, moved,
        already_moved}, rows, total_rows, triggers, bindings, unreached}.
        Exit 4 under the read-only switch, but for --dry-run.
  node list
        The nodes of this project's agent: {ok, project, agent_id, nodes
        [{node, label, kind, address, is_master, may_write, target,
        last_seen_at, coolify_connection, coolify_server, coolify_resource,
        public_key, folder}], store}. A node is a row of the node registry
        (capabilities_nodes): node is its id, the key every store row naming
        the node holds, and label the name it is shown by. is_master,
        may_write and folder belong to the agent's link to it
        (capabilities_agent_nodes): folder is the folder the agent lives in
        on that node, null where it is not recorded.
  node id [--set <id>]
        This machine's node id, resolved with nothing configured: on a Mac
        derived from its hardware UUID through a hash (the UUID never leaves
        it, nothing is stored), elsewhere read from
        $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/node-id, minted there once where it is
        missing; AGENT_NODE_ID (twelve lowercase hex characters, or the name
        a node registered before ids is still keyed by, as a box compiled for
        a deployment target is handed; exit 6 bad_node_id otherwise)
        overrides both. Answers {ok, id, source
        (env | hardware | file | minted), label}, label being the one this
        machine gives itself when it is first registered (AGENT_NODE_NAME,
        else its host name), and, where AGENT_NODE_ID is set, overridden_by
        and warnings [{code node_id_in_environment, variable, value, message,
        hint}]: only a node still keyed by its name from before ids needs it.
        --set <id> writes that file (`deployment node enrol` sets the id a
        server was created with); refused on a Mac (node_id_derived) and
        under the read-only switch. No project is needed.
  node add <id> --label TEXT --kind mac|server [--address HOST]
           [--target NAME] [--master] [--may-write]
           [--coolify-connection ID --coolify-server UUID
            --coolify-resource UUID] [--public-key KEY]
        Register a node of this project's agent, registering the agent first
        where needed. <id> is the id `node id` answers on that machine, or,
        for a machine that predates node ids such as a deployment target's
        server, the name it is known by - letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-'
        (exit 6 input for anything else) - which stands as its id until
        `node rekey` on that machine moves it, and is its label unless
        --label names another; a node the registry already holds - another
        agent's - is linked as it is, and --label and --kind are needed only
        for a new one (--label defaults to this machine's own where <id> is
        this machine). --address is its fixed host name or IP,
        --target the deployment target that deploys it. A node that is a
        Coolify resource names the coolify connection it was created
        through, its server and the resource, all three or none (exit 6
        coolify_incomplete); the token stays in that connection.
        --public-key is the node's X25519 public key, its 32 bytes in base64
        (`deployment node enrol` records it). An agent has at most one
        master: --master while another node is master exits 6 master_exists
        naming it. A node already registered exits 6 node_exists. Answers
        {ok, project, agent_id, node, changed, store}.
  node set <node> [--label TEXT] [--kind mac|server] [--address HOST]
           [--target NAME] [--master | --no-master]
           [--may-write | --no-may-write] [--coolify-connection ID]
           [--coolify-server UUID] [--coolify-resource UUID]
           [--public-key KEY]
        Change a registered node's fields; an empty value clears one, but a
        label is never empty. <node> is its id, or a label exactly one of
        the agent's nodes carries (node_ambiguous otherwise). The label,
        kind, address, target, Coolify resource and key are the node's own,
        in its registry row, so renaming a node changes that one row and no
        row that names it; master and may-write are the agent's link's.
        Exit 6 node_unknown for a node not registered, master_exists as for
        add. Answers as add, changed false when nothing moved.
  node remove <node>
        Remove a registered node from this agent - its link, and its
        registry row once no agent lives on it; answers {ok, project,
        agent_id, removed, store}. Exit 6 node_unknown for a node not
        registered.
  node seen
        Report this node up: set its last_seen_at to the store's now. The
        node is the one this process runs on (`node id`); a node reports
        itself and no other. Every `deployment runner` runs it on each
        reconcile, `node run` does the same, and `deployment help` says
        when a node counts as live.
        Answers {ok, project, agent_id, node, last_seen_at, store}. Exit 6
        node_unknown for a node not registered.
  node rekey <old id> [<new id>] [--dry-run]
        Move a node from the id it is known by - a node registered before
        ids keeps the name it had as its id - to its own: this machine's id
        (`node id`), or the one given. In one transaction the registry row
        takes the new id, every agent's link to it follows, and every column
        of the store that names a node moves with it: the policy, grant,
        identifier and settings rows for that node, the service leases,
        sessions, runs, definitions, executions and memory entries recorded
        under it (only the node part of agentrooms' lease holder); the
        tables' triggers that would refuse it are held off inside that
        transaction, and it rolls back where a row still names the old id
        (exit 5 rekey_incomplete). Where the new id is registered already -
        this machine registered itself before the rekey - that row takes the
        links and the old row's label and fields. It refuses, changing
        nothing, while a live lease names the old id (exit 6
        node_services_running): stop those services first. Run again it
        changes nothing (store.already_moved). Exit 6 node_unknown where the
        store holds neither id. --dry-run changes nothing and answers the
        same with every row it would move. Answers {ok, project, dry_run,
        old_id, new_id, label, merged, rows [{table, column, rows, how}],
        total_rows, triggers, store {level, schema, moved, already_moved}}.
        The agent and node verbs but `node id` need the project's id (exit 6
        no_project_identity: run init) and a database that resolves for the
        project (exit 6 store_not_configured, 5 when it cannot be reached);
        set, add, remove, seen and rekey exit 4 under the read-only switch,
        but for rekey --dry-run.
  node run --supervisor <dir> [--every N] [--services <name,...>]
        The node's service manager: one per node and OS user, run as a
        program of the Supervisor whose configuration includes <dir>/*.conf
        (it reaches that Supervisor through SUPERVISOR_SERVER_URL, which
        Supervisor sets). For every agent the store links to this node
        whose folder here is bound to it, and every service the agent places
        on this node (`place`), it keeps one Supervisor program in <dir>,
        named <capability>.<agent id>, running `node supervise` in the
        agent's folder; a service no longer placed here loses its program.
        A service with no project command that declares a machine one
        (service.machine) has one program for the node instead, named
        <capability>.machine, running `node supervise <name> --machine` in
        this folder while any agent here places it.
        --services limits it to those capabilities, the ones this
        Supervisor's box carries; a service placed here outside them is left
        to whatever runs it, such as a container of its own.
        It applies the difference with `supervisorctl update`, which leaves
        every unchanged program running, so node run can stop and start
        again while the services keep running. It reconciles every N
        seconds (default 60, 5 to 3600) and within seconds of a placement
        change, announced by the manager's placement writes, and answers a
        `service restart` for one of its programs. It reports the node seen
        on each reconcile. While the store cannot be read it changes no job,
        and at its first start it writes none until it has read the store. A
        placed service that cannot run here - not installed, no deploy
        command, quarantined, or its agent's folder not bound to it - is
        named once on stderr. Exit 6 supervisor_required,
        supervisor_unreachable or node_run_running.
  node run --launchd [--every N] [--services <name,...>]
        The same on a Mac, where each job is a launchd agent of this OS user
        instead: ~/Library/LaunchAgents/capabilities.<agent slug>.<capability>
        .plist (the agent's id for a slug of `machine`, deployment's machine
        scope), whose program is a launcher of the same name running `node
        supervise` in the agent's folder, with its logs beside the launcher in
        $XDG_STATE_HOME/capabilities/node/launchd/ and the node manager's
        PATH, node id and home variables; a machine service's one job is
        capabilities.node-machine.<capability>. Each job is loaded, replaced and
        taken down on its own (`launchctl bootstrap`, `bootout`), a job that
        is not loaded is loaded again on the next reconcile, and a restart is
        `launchctl kickstart -k`. The jobs are launchd's, so node run can stop
        and start again while every service keeps running. `node install`
        loads it. Exit 6 launchd_unavailable where launchctl is not on PATH.
  node install [--apply]
        Install this Mac's node manager, once per OS user: the launchd agent
        capabilities.node, running `node run --launchd` in this folder with
        the PATH this shell has, at every login. Before it loads, every
        folder whose services it will run - this agent's and each agent the
        store links to this node whose folder here is bound to it - hands its
        old host agents over (`deployment handover` there), so no service
        runs twice. A service a loaded old agent runs that nothing places -
        an exclusive one placed on no node, a shared one not on this one - is
        first placed on this node (`capabilities place <name> --node <this
        node>` there), so the migration stops no service that was running;
        where that fails the folder's agents are not handed over. Without
        --apply it changes nothing and answers what it would do, which is how
        a machine's migration is announced before it runs. Answers {applied,
        project, agent_id, node, served [{agent_id, folder}], left [{agent_id,
        folder, reason}], handover [{agent_id, folder, agents, placed? |
        would_place? [{service, node, placement}], error?}], node_agent
        {label, plist, program, folder, changed, loaded}, store, next?}. Run
        again it changes nothing. Exit 6
        launchd_unavailable, agent_not_on_node (run `capabilities init`
        here), node_agent_not_loaded, or handover_incomplete, with the answer
        as the error's detail; --apply exits 4 under the read-only switch.
  node supervise <name> [--agent <id>] [--every N] [--restart POLICY]
                  [-- <command...>]
        Run one capability service as the gate on this node says, until
        stopped: what a node-run program runs, what a box's Supervisor
        program and a service's own Compose container run, and what `deployment
        runner` hands its service to. Without a command it runs <name>'s
        deploy command and restart policy from its installed descriptor;
        --every defaults to the project's service_policy.reconcile_seconds in
        deployment/runtime.json (default 60, 5-3600), --restart to the
        descriptor's or unless-stopped (no|on-failure|always|unless-stopped).
        Every N seconds it reads the gate for <name> in this project on this
        node: in store mode only the project's row naming this node places the
        service, by its service flag (`place|unplace`), apart from whether
        that row makes the CLI usable here; in files mode the project's own
        enable does, and nothing inherited or global places it. Placed, an
        exclusive service - the default, as its descriptor's
        service.placement declares - takes or renews the service's lease
        (deployment_service_leases, the table deployment's runner kept, made
        where the store holds none yet), a row in the project's
        store naming the holding node and expiring after three reconciles, so
        it runs on one node at a time; a shared service takes no lease and
        runs on every node it is placed on. Either way it starts the command
        in a process group of its own and restarts it per the policy; a
        service that exits 4, the contract's policy refusal, is not
        restarted: the lease is released and the service held idle, reporting
        the refusal (its error code and message when it printed one), until
        the gate answer for this node changes or the supervisor restarts. A
        service that exits with any other non-zero code, or cannot be started,
        has its exit code, its last stderr lines (at most 2000 characters) and
        the time written onto the lease it holds; another node taking the
        lease clears them. Not placed, or the lease held live by another node,
        it stops the group (TERM, KILL after 20 seconds) and releases its
        lease; a refusal names the holder and expiry on stderr, and it keeps
        checking, so it takes over once the lease expires or is released.
        With no database resolvable no lease can be taken, and it runs as the
        gate says. Each reconcile it also reports this node seen (`node
        seen`); a report that fails is said once on stderr and changes nothing
        else. While the service is placed here it keeps this node's row in
        capabilities_service_instances: its state - starting, ready, unusable,
        degraded, failing, held, waiting or stopping - with the problem and
        its code, the payload, pid, host and start time of the running
        process, the last failure's exit code, stderr tail and time, and the
        settings skipped on this node. Whether a running service can do its
        work is its declared doctor's answer (service.deploy.doctor), asked
        10 seconds after it starts and then every reconcile, for at most 30
        seconds: exit 0 is ready, or degraded when its body names
        skipped_settings; any other answer is unusable, with the message and
        code of the error envelope it printed, else the body's problem, else
        its exit code's category. The row goes when the service is no longer
        placed here or the supervisor stops; a project that declares no id
        keeps none. It records its state as deployment always kept a runner's
        (capabilities/deployment/state/runners/<name>.json, read by `deployment
        services`), and a second supervisor of the same service in the same
        project on this machine - a host agent loaded again, a runner of an
        older release - starts nothing and waits for the first to stop. The
        service gets each environment default the compiled service carries -
        its descriptor's optional defaults, then the project runtime's
        environment_defaults for it - where the supervisor's environment sets
        none. Exit 6 agent_mismatch where the folder is bound to another agent
        than --agent, 3 no_service; 4 under the read-only switch.
  node supervise --probe
        Answer {supervises: true} and start nothing: how `deployment runner`
        learns that this manager supervises the service it is handed, where an
        older one refuses the option and the runner supervises it itself.
  node supervise <name> --machine [--every N] [--restart POLICY]
        What a node-run job runs for a service with no project command that
        declares one per machine (service.machine.command): one process on
        this node for every agent here that places it. Each reconcile it reads
        those agents from the store, takes each one's lease where the service
        is exclusive, puts each agent whose lease it holds on the service's own
        list (`<name> service join` in its folder) and takes off those it put
        there that it no longer holds (`service leave`), and runs the machine
        command while any agent is on it, under the restart policy and exit-4
        hold above, probing it with service.machine.doctor. It writes one row
        per placed agent: from what `<name> service status --machine` says of
        that agent (served ready, paused held, refused or error unusable),
        waiting for an agent whose lease another node holds. Where a machine
        process it did not start runs <name> here - an agent of `deployment
        machine sync`, or one started by hand - it starts none beside it,
        changes no list and writes each row waiting. Stopping, it stops the
        process and takes off the list every agent it put there. Its record
        is under $XDG_STATE_HOME/capabilities/node/machine/.
  service start <name> [--node <node>] [--wait S]
  service move <name> --node <node> [--wait S]
  service stop <name> [--node <node> | --all] [--wait S]
  service restart <name> [--node <node>] [--wait S]
  service status [<name>] [--agent <id|slug>]
        One set of handles for every capability's service, in a store-mode
        project; the node defaults to this one. start places the service on
        the node (as `place --node`: an exclusive one leaves every other
        node in the same write) and waits up to S seconds (default 120, 0 to
        3600) for that node's row in capabilities_service_instances: it
        answers {started, node, placement, changed, placed_on, ready, state,
        instance, waited_seconds, unplaced_from?} once the row is ready or
        degraded, and exits 6 service_<state> (unusable, held, failing,
        waiting, starting, or absent for no row) with that answer as the
        error's detail when it is not. move does the same for an exclusive
        service (exit 6 placement_shared otherwise) and also waits for every
        other node's row to go. stop unplaces it from the node, or with
        --all from every node it is placed or running on, and waits until
        those nodes hold no row: {stopped, nodes, changed, placed_on,
        waited_seconds}, else exit 6 service_still_running. restart asks the
        node's manager through the store to restart its program and waits
        for the row's started_at to move: {restarted, node, state, ready,
        instance, waited_seconds}; exit 6 service_not_running where the node
        has no row, restart_unanswered where nothing restarted it (only
        `node run` answers). status writes nothing: {ok, project, agent_id,
        this_node, services [{capability, placed_on, ready_on, instances
        [row]}], store}, each row with its node, state, ready, problem,
        problem_code, payload_sha256, pid, host, started_at, checked_at,
        checked_seconds_ago, last_exit_code, last_error, last_error_at and
        skipped_settings; --agent reads another agent in the same store.
        start, move and stop exit 4 under the read-only switch.
  context --claude|--codex|--fragment
        Compose the session injection from the gate + snapshots + project
        envelopes. --fragment prints the manager-owned Markdown block without
        writing host files; context owners such as ContextKit insert this output
        verbatim. The host modes place the same block where the host reads it
        (--claude: .claude/rules/CAPABILITIES.md; --codex:
        .codex/generated/capabilities.md, which Codex loads through the root
        .capabilities.md link named by project_doc_fallback_filenames in
        .codex/config.toml) and replace the file whole. In a Codex-wired
        project --codex also creates or repairs that link and config entry,
        replacing the manager's earlier fallback names and keeping others.
        Composition reads the active records backend and degradation is
        per-capability, never whole. A capability whose manifest declares a
        `contribution` has that file, read from its own state folder in the
        project envelope and held to its declared byte ceiling, composed after
        its stub line; a contribution that is missing, unreadable, malformed
        or over its ceiling is replaced by one line naming `<name> render`,
        and costs nothing else. Directly above a composed contribution of
        `memory`, and nowhere else, the block carries the line
        `<!-- contextkit:memory-contribution -->`, on which ContextKit leaves
        out its own projection of the project's memory folder. Run by hand, a
        host mode prints the file it wrote and the enabled set. Run as the host's SessionStart hook (the
        harness pipes a payload whose hook_event_name is SessionStart), it
        prints nothing unless the session must be told: the file was just
        created or changed since the session loaded it, it could not be
        rebuilt, or Codex cannot load it because a root AGENTS.md or
        AGENTS.override.md takes precedence. The hook always exits 0, and the
        context reaches the agent through the host's own file loading, never
        through hook output. The generated agent instruction paragraph
        defaults to identifier persistence guidance and can be overridden with
        CAPABILITIES_CONTEXT_PROMPT in the project's .env/.env.local.
        Do not create or edit generated context files by hand; rerun this
        command after manual envelope changes. In a ContextKit-managed project
        (`.contextkit/config.toml`), ContextKit owns both host bindings and
        the combined context; the host modes delegate without writing and
        point to `contextkit build`, while --fragment remains the read-only
        provider surface ContextKit calls.
  build [--print] [--json]
        Compose an agent's generated host context - a ContextKit-bound
        agent's, or one bound by its project id alone - in the
        shape `contextkit build` gives it: runtime and collaboration texts
        that route the agent to this manager and to the `context`, `assets`
        and `routine` capabilities, a guide menu of only the guides those
        own, onboarding, foreign instruction files, inline and stub context
        (global context included), routine index and this manager's own
        capabilities block, whose refresh line names `build`, composed in
        this process. When the agent keeps
        context documents in its store (the `context` capability's tables),
        the inline and stub context is composed from their active versions,
        read-only, instead of the context folder - global documents too when
        the binding names global context and the store holds them - with a
        warning while the folder is still there; a store that does not answer,
        or a stored document that does not read, leaves the folder in use with
        a warning. Likewise, when the agent keeps routines in its store (the
        `routine` capability's tables), the routine index lists their active
        versions by name, each pointing to `routine show <name>`, instead of
        the routines folder, and the onboarding block points at its routine
        the same way, with a warning while the folder is still there; a
        store that does not answer leaves the folder in use with a warning.
        An agent without a ContextKit binding reads its body root,
        collaboration mode and onboarding from its row (`agent show`), keeps
        its layers at their own names in that body root, and has no global
        context folder. --print writes the text
        to stdout and writes nothing; with --json it answers {project, output,
        text, warnings}. Without --print it replaces the output - for a
        ContextKit-bound agent the configured one (`[output] context`,
        default .contextkit/generated/context.md), otherwise
        .capabilities-generated/context.md - whole
        and points each host that has a hook for it at it (.contextkit.md,
        .claude/rules/CONTEXT.md), leaving a path that holds something else
        blocked; it answers {project, output, created, changed, links,
        foreign_instructions, warnings}. An output that is not generated
        context exits 6 output_not_generated. No update notice is composed.
        A project with neither a ContextKit binding nor a project id exits 6
        not_contextkit_bound.
  session-start --host claude|codex
        What an agent's SessionStart hook runs: rebuilds the
        generated context as `build` does and prints a notice to the host only
        when the session must be told - the context was just created or its
        host link just made, it changed since the session loaded it, it could
        not be rebuilt, or the host cannot load it (Codex: a root AGENTS.md or
        AGENTS.override.md, or .contextkit.md not linked; Claude:
        .claude/rules/CONTEXT.md not linked). It always exits 0 and the
        context reaches the agent through the host's own file loading. In a
        project with neither a ContextKit binding nor a project id it gives
        the notice `context` gives as a hook.
  hooks install [--claude] [--codex]
        In an agent's project - ContextKit-bound, or bound by its project id
        alone - point the SessionStart hooks at
        `capabilities session-start`: the Claude hook in .claude/settings.json
        becomes `/bin/bash -lc 'capabilities session-start --host claude'`
        (a new settings file also gets autoMemoryEnabled false), and the Codex
        hook keeps its entry in .codex/hooks.json - the one Codex trusts by
        hash - while its script .codex/hooks/build-context.sh runs
        `capabilities session-start --host codex`. For Codex it also sets
        project_doc_fallback_filenames to list .contextkit.md and
        project_doc_max_bytes to at least 524288 in .codex/config.toml, and
        records the folder and hook trust in $CODEX_HOME/config.toml (default
        ~/.codex). Every other key and hook is left as it is; a duplicate
        session-start hook is dropped. Without a ContextKit binding it also
        retires the standalone capabilities wiring of the hosts it wires
        (the `capabilities context` hooks, .claude/rules/CAPABILITIES.md, the
        .capabilities.md link and its generated file) and writes the
        manager's block naming the host files it writes
        (.capabilities-generated/, .contextkit.md, .claude/settings.json,
        .claude/rules/CONTEXT.md, .codex/config.toml, .codex/hooks.json,
        .codex/hooks/) into the root .gitignore where it is missing. It then
        builds the context and links
        the hosts. Without a flag it takes over the hosts already wired (exit
        6 no_host_wired when none is). Rerunning changes nothing. Answers
        {project, hosts, codex, claude, build}, with retired_capabilities_wiring
        and gitignore {path, changed} without a binding. Exit 6
        not_contextkit_bound with neither a binding nor a project id,
        host_settings_unreadable for a host file it cannot
        read, codex_config_blocked where the Codex settings cannot be edited
        safely.
  hooks check
        Report, as JSON, whether each wired host of an agent's project
        delivers its context, wired by ContextKit or by `hooks install`:
        {ok, project, output, hosts, foreign_instructions, problems}. Per
        host: the hook present once and running a program found on PATH
        (`runs`, `program`), the host link resolving to the generated context,
        and for Codex the adapter script, the project-doc settings and byte
        budget, root AGENTS files and the trust records. Exit 7 when any
        problem is found; 6 not_contextkit_bound with neither a binding nor
        a project id.
  inventory [NAME ...] [--detail] [--network]
        What this project's capabilities are and what they hold. One row per
        effective-enabled capability: its tier (as in `list`), gate and the
        scope that set it,
        the connections it may act on and the ones a grant withholds,
        the declared service and its state, and whether the
        capability reports an inventory of its own and how much of it needs
        the network. The survey runs only the capabilities that ship a
        service, each once for its service's state from its own local report
        (`unknown`, with the reason, when it cannot answer). A service row
        carries `state` (`running` while a process serves the project, its
        own or the machine service it joined), `detail`, and the verdict of
        the service's local checks: `ok` true when they find nothing wrong,
        false with a one-line `problem` when they do, null when the capability
        gives no verdict. The checks are the local part of its `service
        doctor`, with no network call and no model turn. --detail also asks
        each one for its own report — `<name> inventory`, local and effect-free
        — running them together, so a reader assembling a whole project spends
        one process per capability here instead of several in every caller.
        --network implies --detail and passes the caller's consent down, so a
        capability may then reach its service for detail a local read cannot
        supply. NAME limits the answer to named capabilities.
        `list` answers for the machine; this answers for the project.
  ids <name>
        Render <name>'s project identifiers as markdown — the full labelled
        list the session context points to. (`<name> ids get|set|rm` is the
        per-value data interface on the capability itself.)
  references list [<name>] | show <name> <ref> [--version ID]
             | history <name> <ref>
             | save <name> <ref> --from FILE|- [--base ID] [--note TEXT]
             | retire <name> <ref> | import [<name>...] | export [<name>...]
        This project's references in its store: every capability's, in one
        versioned table, capabilities_references, keyed by project,
        capability and name. A reference the store holds is what `<name>
        refs` and `<name> context show|edit|put` read and write, before any
        file of its key; one it does not hold is read from
        capabilities/<name>/reference/ as before, and so is every reference
        where no store answers. import reads each
        capabilities/<name>/reference/*.md, of the capabilities named or of
        all, byte for byte (UTF-8) as one version: a new reference, a new
        version where the active one differs, nothing where it is equal;
        every file is checked first and the whole import is written in one
        transaction, or nothing is (exit 6 import_refused, naming each
        file). export writes each active reference back to
        capabilities/<name>/reference/<ref>.md byte for byte, leaving a file
        already equal alone, and names under files_left the files the store
        does not hold. save adds a version from FILE or stdin; --base names
        the version (its id, or a prefix of six or more characters) the edit
        was made from, and the save is refused (exit 6 stale_base) when
        another is active now; a body equal to the active one adds nothing
        ("changed": false). history lists every version, newest first; show
        prints the active body, or the one --version names, exactly; retire
        leaves a reference no active version, keeping every version, so its
        file is read again. A write stamps the project id, verified; every
        verb but list, show and history is exit 4 under the read-only switch,
        and 5 where the store cannot be reached.
  get <name> <collection> <key>
        Read one resolved configuration record from wherever this project keeps
        its records. Collections are connection, grant, identifier, setting,
        policy, and document. In store mode a grant is the row that applies on
        this node - the one naming it, else the one for every node - and adds
        node (null for every node) and is_default to its fields.
  set <name> <collection> <key> <json-or-string> [--note TEXT]
        Write one project-scoped human-owned configuration record. The writable
        collections here are connection, grant, and setting; policy stays with
        the policy verbs, identifiers with the capability's ids verbs, and long
        text with the capability's context verbs. In store mode a connection
        is a row every project may be granted, a grant is this project's row
        and may carry node (a node id, or null for every node, the default)
        and is_default (the default pointer, one per capability and node), and
        setting connection.default moves is_default; such a write stamps the
        project id, so it refuses (exit 6) where the id is not verified, and
        `records.mode` answers `store` with the table as `source`. A store
        that cannot be reached is exit 5 store_unreachable.
  connections list [<name> ...]
        Every connection the store holds, per capability - of the capabilities
        named, or of all - with its owner and every grant on it, read from the
        database `store show` names here, inside a project or outside one. A
        connection is shared by the capability (owner null, shared true),
        which any agent may be granted, or one agent's own (owner its id),
        its own account, granted to no other agent; a connection added with
        `<name> connection add --project` in a project in store mode is that
        project's own, written with its grant. Each grant names its agent
        (null: the grant for every agent), node (null: every node), enabled,
        allow_write (null falls to the capability's WRITE_DEFAULT) and
        is_default; a registered agent's name rides beside its id. Reads only.
        JSON {store {schema}, capabilities {<name> [{connection, owner,
        owner_name, shared, grants [{agent, agent_name, node, enabled,
        allow_write, is_default}]}]}}. Exit 6 tables_behind where the store's
        tables predate owners, 5 when the store cannot be reached.
  connections import
        Move this project's records - connections, grants, identifiers and
        policy - from its files into its store (the database `store show`
        names for it) and the project into store mode, recorded as
        "records": "store" in its older project.json, which only this verb
        writes (a project without one, bound to its id with a store, is in
        store mode already);
        a project at "connections": "store" keeps only its connections and
        grants in the store, and its identifiers and policy in files, until
        this verb runs again. Every identity the project uses becomes a row
        of capabilities_connections: each one its envelope declares, and an
        identity of the config home only where the capability is enabled for
        the project and the project may use that identity or points its
        default at it. One the config home merely offers stays out of the
        store and is named under skipped; once the project uses it, the
        import run again from its files writes it, and a project already in
        store mode writes it with `set <name> connection <id> <spec>` and
        `set <name> grant <id> '{"enabled": true}'`. Every row written that
        the project may use, switched off itself, or points its default at
        gets a grant for every node in capabilities_connection_grants,
        stamped with the project id (verified, as any write that stamps it),
        carrying the decision its files resolve to (a field no scope decided
        stays null). Rows already in the store are never removed.
        The rows are written, and proven to resolve as the files do, in one
        transaction before project.json changes. A row the store already
        holds unchanged is left as it is, so running it again changes
        nothing; one that differs, or a grant of this project the files do
        not have, refuses the whole import (exit 6 import_conflict) and
        writes nothing. Each scope's identifiers and policy entries become
        rows of capabilities_identifiers and capabilities_policy, the
        envelope's under the project id and the config home's as global rows
        (agent_id null, for every project reading the store), and the store
        must resolve this project's identifiers and gate as its files do, each
        from the same scope: a held row that differs, a project row the files
        lack, or a global row that would change what this project resolves
        refuses the import as well. The files are kept and no longer read for
        those records. JSON {ok, project, agent_id, node, mode,
        previous_mode (files, connections or store), identity, store {level,
        sources, schema, tables}, capabilities {<name> {connections
        {imported, unchanged}, grants {imported, unchanged}, default}},
        skipped {<name> [<id>]}, identifiers {<name> {project|global {imported, unchanged}}}, policy
        {project|global {imported, unchanged}}, service {placed, unchanged},
        files_kept}. A capability the project enables itself whose service
        the files ran here stays placed: the import writes the project's row
        naming this node with its service placed (service.placed), as
        `place` would. Exit 4 under the
        read-only switch, 5 when the store cannot be reached.

  migrate [--dry-run|--apply] [--answers FILE]
        Move this agent's file-held configuration into the store, on its
        master node, as one deliberate run: its connections, grants and
        default pointer, identifiers and gate (as rows naming the agent), the
        settings of every capability's project home as settings rows - with
        capabilities/tasks/conveyor.toml folded in as the tasks settings'
        `escalation` table and capabilities/slack/policy.json merged into
        the slack settings' allow-list (an entry both hold keeps the
        service's; each entry only one holds, and each mode the two
        disagree on, asked) - and through each capability's `<name> migrate files` what it keeps
        of its own: automations [[automations]] and engine.timezone as
        definitions and [agents.workers] as harness profiles
        automations-<name>, askproject's profiles folder, whatsapp's and tasks'
        profile files and tasks' worker files as store versions, telegram's
        old worker settings as profiles, agentrooms' agents map as workers,
        and deployment's runtime.json service_policy and placement as policy
        rows naming each node. The default is a dry run, which writes
        nothing: what each part would write, every row the store already
        holds differently, the steps that would bring the contract's tables
        and each capability's own tables to theirs (tables_to_upgrade, by
        owner; a dry run changes nothing in the store, its schema included,
        and reads it as those steps would leave it, a table not made yet as
        empty), each settings value naming a host path, each file
        that differs from its committed copy, each other root of the agent
        on this machine (verify-only, differences named), one node per
        deployment target, and every question code cannot answer - a node
        for a legacy automation, a connection whose stored spec differs
        ("store": the agent adopts the stored spec; "files": the stored spec
        is replaced; {"rename": "<name>"}: the agent's own is written under
        that name and its grants, default and settings `connection` follow
        it, the stored one left to the others), a node to register, an id the
        project's files (.env.local/.env, else project.json v1) and
        .contextkit/config.toml disagree on (the one chosen is written to
        .env.local), a slack user or channel only one of policy.json and the
        service settings holds (keep or drop), a slack mode the two disagree
        on. Each question names the path in the --answers JSON its
        answer goes to. --apply refuses (exit 6, nothing written) without a
        database (its own code), where this machine resolves no node id
        (node_unidentified), on a node other than the answers' (named by its
        id or its label) or the open migration's (node_mismatch)
        or other than the agent's master (not_master), before `migrate
        --global` (global_not_migrated), with a payload older than this
        contract (payload_outdated) or with a question open (question_open).
        A second checkout of the agent - a linked git worktree of its main
        checkout, a root other than the one its open migration started in,
        or a root whose files are behind another root of the same id that
        already keeps the records in the store - only verifies: it names its
        main checkout and differences (second_checkout), writes, removes
        and freezes nothing, and until the agent has moved refuses
        (second_checkout) naming the main checkout. A root in files mode
        whose agent the store already keeps records of, with no checkout of
        it found, refuses the same way and asks `checkout`: answered with
        this root's path, it moves the agent from here.
        It registers the agent and this node as its master on the first
        run, sets migrating_since (from then only this migration writes the
        agent's records and settings, on any node), archives every file git
        does not hold as committed at 0600 under the state home, stops the
        agent's launchd agents here and pauses its services that pause,
        registers the target nodes answered, writes, then reads everything
        back through the store and compares it both ways with what the files
        resolve (exit 7 verify_failed, the agent left frozen). Finalize
        refuses while a target's node is unregistered
        (target_node_unregistered), else sets migrated_at, clears
        migrating_since, gives the agent's row its slug where it has none
        (the one its project.json names, see init), writes "records":
        "store" where an older project.json remains, removes the files (a git
        change to commit) and resumes what it stopped. A journal under the
        state home makes a run interrupted anywhere resume on the next
        --apply; a run on an agent already moved changes nothing. An agent
        whose records were already in the store keeps them: its stale files
        are named, never fail the run, and are removed. One whose project.json
        says "records": "store" while the store in force holds none of its
        grants, identifiers or gate, and its files hold some, is asked
        `records`: "files" writes the files as for an agent in files mode,
        "store" keeps the store and removes them. JSON {ok, project,
        agent_id, node, mode, applied, state, blockers, questions, plan,
        would_write | registered, movers, records_written,
        settings_written, verify, migrated_at, removed, archive, services}.
  migrate --global [--dry-run|--apply] [--answers FILE]
        Write the config home's gate, identifiers, connections, grants (a
        grant's allow_write only, never enabled), default pointer and global
        settings as the global rows every agent inherits, converging the
        store's global rows on them, and set global_migrated_at; the dry run
        lists each row it would add, change or remove and every agent whose
        effective gate would flip (gate_flips), counting only agents that
        read the same database. --apply first brings the contract's tables
        to this code's version and names the steps it ran (tables_upgraded);
        the dry run changes nothing, the schema included, names the steps an
        apply would run (tables_to_upgrade, by owner) and plans as if they
        had run.
        Once set, it changes nothing.
  migrate --discard [--root PATH]...
        On a node that is not the global master: delete the config home's
        record and settings files without reading them. The roots it weighs
        are the ones --report finds plus every root holding an envelope or a
        ContextKit binding below the folders those sit in and below each
        --root. Refused where this machine resolves no node id
        (node_unidentified), on the master
        node of any of those agents (master_node), before global_migrated_at
        (global_not_migrated), or while one of those roots has not moved or
        has no id (agents_unmigrated, each root named with where it was
        found).
  migrate --retire-connections
        On any node, the global master's included, once the store this
        project (outside one, this machine) reads holds the global rows:
        check that every connection of each config-home connections file
        ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/<name>/connections.json) is a row of the store
        with the same fields (its grant fields aside), then move those files
        to $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/retired/connections-<UTC stamp>/
        <name>/connections.json. Credentials and env files, *.session files
        and every other file stay where they are. Refused before
        global_migrated_at (global_not_migrated), and, moving nothing, while
        a file's connection is missing from the store or differs there
        (connections_not_in_store, each named), or a file cannot be read
        (files_unreadable). Run it after `capabilities update`: a capability
        on a contract library older than 0.15 still reads these files. JSON
        {ok, global_migrated_at, config_home, backup, retired [{file,
        backup, connections}]}.
  migrate --retire-records [--root PATH]...
        On any node, the global master's included, once the store this
        project (outside one, this machine) reads holds the global rows:
        check that every value of the config home's gate
        ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/settings.json), identifiers files
        (<name>/identifiers.json) and global settings files (each global
        settings home a capability declares) is a global row of the store
        with the same value, then move those files to
        $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/capabilities/retired/records-<UTC stamp>/, each at
        its path below the config home. Connections files are
        --retire-connections'; credentials and env files, *.session files
        and every other file stay where they are. Refused, moving nothing,
        before global_migrated_at (global_not_migrated), while a value is
        missing from the store or differs there (records_not_in_store, each
        file, capability and key named), while a file cannot be read
        (files_unreadable), or while a root on this machine that may read
        them has not moved or has no id (agents_unmigrated; the roots are
        found as --discard finds them). Run it after `capabilities update`.
        JSON {ok, global_migrated_at, config_home, backup, retired [{file,
        backup}]}.
  migrate --report [--root PATH]...
        What remains on this machine and why: each agent root found in the
        machine services' join lists, AgentKit's agent list, the roots
        given, the project here and their git worktrees, read through each
        one's body root - its state, its record and settings files left, an
        open migration's journal, and the lines of its own text that read
        those files directly or name a retired import verb (consumers) apart
        from historical notes that only quote them - assets/, memory/ and
        snapshots/ at the root or body root, and change logs - which are
        counted (historical_notes) and left as they are - and the config
        home's files.

VERIFICATION VERBS
  doctor
        Reconcile machine registry ↔ snapshots ↔ project gate ↔ gitignore
        guard ↔ deny rules ↔ standalone host wiring, including what keeps
        Codex from loading the capability context ↔ project identity; report
        drift, and the project's project_identity state (see path
        --identity), which moves an id still kept in project.json into
        .env.local. warnings names AGENT_NODE_ID set in the environment
        (node_id_in_environment), as `node id` does; it is not drift. A
        store-mode project whose store cannot be used is drift. In store mode `information` lists, never
        as drift, each agent_id holding grants in the store that ContextKit's
        registry (contextkit_agents) does not know (grant_agent_unknown), or
        that the registry is not in the store (agents_registry_absent).
        Exit 0 clean, 7 drift found.
  audit <name> [--from <path>]
        Contract conformance: call the contract verbs, validate output
        shapes, exercise the gate (enabled / disabled / absent) in a
        sandbox, and check that the script binds the shared code from the
        capabilities-contract library by a range this manager supports
        (contract-range). Exit 0 pass, 7 failures found.
  selfcheck
        Prove the repo's own wholeness (the inward companion to audit): the
        enforcer parses, both audit branches (core-only / connection-bearing)
        have a living green example and fire on a deliberate break, every standing
        rule names an enforcement the manager has, and every doc link resolves.
        Fleet capability audit remains a direct dev/source check.
        Exit 0 whole, 7 otherwise.

AUTHORING
  new <name> --source ID [--core-only]
        Create a canonical capability skeleton in that source; it binds the
        shared code from the capabilities-contract library by the range this
        manager supports.
  guide [<topic>]
        Without a topic, list manager guides with their titles, previews, and
        exact read commands. With a topic, print that guide's Markdown body.
        Start here for capability authoring, repositories, contract work,
        development, review, sanitizing, and publishing, and for moving
        agents from files to the database (migrate-to-database).

I/O CONTRACT
  Success: JSON on stdout, except the documented Markdown renderers
  (`context --fragment`, `ids`). Failure: {"error": {"code", "message",
  "hint"?}} on stderr. Exit codes: 0 success, 2 auth, 3 not found,
  4 policy (a capability disabled by the gate, or a connection that does not
  allow writes — ask the user, never lift either yourself), 5 network,
  6 input/validation, 7 drift or conformance failures (doctor/audit).
"""

